window.docContents[45].resolve({"/Design-restrictions/Types-and-declarations/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions--Types-and-declarations":{"contents":"C++17 has five standard signed integer types, and int has the natural size of the architecture (N4659 §6.9.1 ¶2). A bool converts to an int by an integral promotion (N4659 §7.6 ¶6), so g++ accepts int x = true;.Core C++ has the basic types int, bool and void. An int is 32 bit two's complement, and its overflow is error, . The relation  is the identity on the basic types, so Core C++ rejects int x = true;, .The restriction fixes the meaning of every arithmetic operation independently of the machine. It also removes every conversion between basic types. So the choice of an overload needs no ranking of conversion sequences, .\n\nIn C++17 an automatic object without an initialiser has an indeterminate value, and an evaluation that produces it has undefined behaviour, with some exceptions (N4659 §11.6 ¶12). A program may also declare variables outside every function. The program int main() { int x; x = 1; return x; } compiles with g++.Core C++ requires an initialiser in every local declaration, by the grammar, . It has no global variables. The parser rejects the program above and every declaration of a variable outside a function.The restriction gives every location a value from its allocation, so the store never holds an indeterminate value. The environment of a function body binds only its parameters, , and the store holds no location before main runs.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Design restrictions","header":"9.2. Types and declarations","id":"/Design-restrictions/Types-and-declarations/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions--Types-and-declarations"},"/Type-systems/Operator-members/#Core-C______--Type-systems--Operator-members":{"contents":"An infix operator whose left operand is an object, and the indexing of an object, are the calls of members. The left operand decides, so no operator on int or bool changes meaning, and && and || are not overloaded.The type checker rewrites both forms into the call of the member, and from there they are ordinary method calls, with the visibility, the overload resolution and the dispatch of a method. The trace shows the rule MethodCall under the infix form.\n\nA member may return , with  not void. Every return e of its body then has an operand that denotes a location under , the context of this and of the parameters, without the locals of the body. The walk does not enter the body of a lambda, because the return of a lambda is the lambda's. The rule T-Ret of the body then requires .The rule T-LocMethod also covers e->m(…), with the selection of T-MethodArrow.The annotation rewrites the return e of such a member as return &e, the location as a value, an expression no program writes. Its type is the type of the location it denotes.Since the locals of the body are outside , a member never returns the location of one of them. The return frees the location of a parameter by value, so a member that returns it returns a freed location. Reading that location has no derivation, an error of the evaluator by the general rule of locations outside .\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Type systems","header":"7.2. Operator members","id":"/Type-systems/Operator-members/#Core-C______--Type-systems--Operator-members"}});